  "full_response": "A Kubernetes cluster is a group of machines that work together to run containerized applications. A cluster typically consists of three types of nodes: the control plane node, the worker nodes, and the database. Each component in cluster serves a different purpose in managing and running containerized workloads.\n\nControl Plane Node:\nThe control plane node, also called the master node, acts as the brain of the Kubernetes cluster. It manages the overall scheduling, cluster management, and orchestration functions. The Kubernetes control plane uses several processes to manage the cluster, including the kube-apiserver, the kube-scheduler, the kube-controller-manager, and etcd.\n\n1. Kubeiserver: The kube-apiserver is the component that every component of the Kubernetes cluster, including the control plane and the node plugins, talk to. It receives, validates, and processes REST requests, then updates the Kubernetes state as needed.\n\n2. Kube-scheduler: The kube-scheduler receives and watches for new, unscheduled pods and selects the appropriate worker node for those pods to run on.\n\n3. Kube-controller-manager: The kube-controller-manager runs multiple minor controllers like the replication controller, the endpoint controller, and the namespace controller. It ensures the desired state specified by the user matches the current state.\n\n4. etcd: The etcd is a key-value store that serves as the primary location of configuration information for the entire Kubernetes cluster.\n\nWorker Node:\nThe worker nodes are the machines in the cluster that run the containerized applications. Each worker node consists of a set of worker processes, including Kubelet, Kube-proxy, and Container Runtime Interface (CRI).\n\n1. Kubelet: The kubelet is the primary \"node agent\" that runs on each worker node and ensures that all the containers specified in the pod's spec are running in the container runtime.\n\n2. Kube-proxy: Kube-proxy is a network proxy that runs on each worker node and provides networking and load balancing for inter-pod communication as well as exposing services to the external world.\n\nContainer Runtime Interface (CRI):\nThe Container Runtime Interface (CRI) is an interface for running containerized workloads. Kubernetes supports arbitrary container runtimes using this interface. The Kubernetes project also provides a universally-deployed container runtime called containerd, which implements the [Kubernetes CRI](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/api/federation/v1beta1/containerservice/types.go).\n\nFinally, the Kubernetes also requires a Storage Class to store pre-provisioned",
